
# The spec-format shape, written in aontu — the same language the corpus
# itself is written in.
#
# This is used by tools/check-spec-shape.js, which unifies it with each
# spec SOURCE and discards the result: the question asked is only "does
# the corpus unify with the shape", and a violation is an ordinary aontu
# conflict, reported with an error code, the exact spec path, and the
# source file it came from.
#
# It is deliberately NOT unified into the corpus during `make spec`. Under
# aontu 0.48.2 an optional key holding an empty container (`out: []`) is
# treated as unresolved and DROPPED from the output, which would silently
# rewrite entries like fib/seq#empty into "no out" - a different test.
# Keeping the check separate from the build keeps the artifact exact.
#
# WHAT THIS ENFORCES (see DOCS.md 2.7):
#   - entry fields limited to the nine defined ones - the typo class, where
#     `matches:` silently replaces `match:` and the assertion disappears
#   - field types: id and client are strings, doc is boolean, err is a
#     boolean or a string; `id: null` is rejected here too
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT ENFORCE, and why:
#   - at most one of in/args/ctx, and `err` never beside `out`: cross-field
#     rules needing `must()`, which aontu 0.48.2 does not implement (Band B
#     of its constraint algebra)
#   - a non-empty `set`: needs the `length()` sizing atom, likewise absent
# The runner enforces all three at run time in every port, and remains the
# authority on spec semantics. When aontu gains `must` and `length` they
# belong here, so that a malformed spec fails at build rather than at test.


# The spec metadata block. `version` is what turns on strict entry
# validation in every runner, so a spec that loses or mistypes it does not
# fail loudly - it silently downgrades the checking in 23 ports at once.
# Closed, so a misspelled `versoin:` is an unknown key rather than a
# second marker nobody reads.
#
# `version` is pinned to the one defined version rather than to `integer`:
# a bump is a decision about every runner's baseline, and it should have to
# be made here as well as in the spec. Update both when version 2 exists.
#
# `requires` is optional and unconstrained - it names capabilities beyond
# the version baseline, and the runner is the authority on those.
#
# ABSENCE is NOT caught here, and cannot be: unifying `{}` with
# `{version: 1}` fills the key in rather than objecting. That case is
# checked in tools/build-spec.js against the built artifact, which is what
# every port actually reads.
Meta: hide(close({
  version: 1
  requires?: top
}))


# One test entry. Closed: an unrecognised key is an error, because an
# unrecognised key is almost always an assertion that stopped asserting.
# Every field is optional - the runner decides which combinations mean
# what - and the data-carrying fields hold arbitrary spec values, so they
# are constrained to presence, not type.
Entry: hide(close({
  in?: top
  args?: top
  ctx?: top
  out?: top
  err?: boolean | string
  match?: top
  client?: string
  id?: string
  doc?: boolean
}))


# One group: a `set` of entries. `set` is optional so that this template
# can be spread across a whole section, where DEF - metadata, not a group -
# sits beside the groups and must pass through untouched.
Group: hide({
  set?: [ &: $.Entry ]
})
